Z||000004||William Federle||09-04-2000||08:34 AM||||I know it is on the LH side. The caliper on the RH side already had the rubber seal on it so I didn't actually see it on that side.<P>The thing is, the springs don't seem to be strong enough to push the levers back against the stop on either side. They seemed pretty stiff when I struggled to get them installed. I can pull the levers back against the stops with a pair of pliers but it seems to me that the springs should push them without help.<P>    I've looked around (the fiero store and autozone web sites) for new springs, but I don't think anyone carries them.<P><BR><p>[This message has been edited by William Federle (edited 09-04-2000).]||||reg||1
Z||000005||William Federle||09-15-2000||01:55 PM||||I was wrong about the front cable moving freely. So I ordered one from the Fierostore and installed it. The thing is that the old one was installed with the retainer on the ebrake handle assem. pointing forward. My new cable doesn't reach that far. But when I installed it with the retainer pointing toward the rear of the car (like it shows in the manual) it's a little too long. With the adjustment tightened all the way, it will cause the brake to hold, but when I release it the wire hangs loose at the equalizer.<P>Has anyone else run into this, and did you find some way to fix it?<BR>||||reg||1
